                "patch": "Index: docs/ONBOARDING.md\n===================================================================\n--- docs/ONBOARDING.md\t\n+++ docs/ONBOARDING.md\t\n@@ -1,273 +1,216 @@\n # Physics Job Hunter — Onboarding Guide\n \n+> Generated from knowledge graph (analyzed 2026-06-05, commit `3cc9d72`)\n+\n ## Project Overview\n \n-**Physics Job Hunter** is an automated job search pipeline that scrapes international-school Physics teaching positions, pre-filters relevant ones, scores them via a weighted multi-provider LLM engine, and sends ranked Telegram alerts with personalised cover letters.\n-\n | Attribute | Value |\n |-----------|-------|\n-| Languages | Python, TOML, JSON, SQL, Markdown |\n-| Frameworks | httpx, BeautifulSoup4, Playwright, APScheduler, Supabase, Pydantic |\n-| Entrypoints | `python3 -m src.main` (CLI) or APScheduler (daily runs) |\n-| License | Proprietary |\n+| **Name** | physics-job-hunter |\n+| **Languages** | Python, TOML, JSON, SQL, Markdown |\n+| **Frameworks** | httpx, BeautifulSoup4, Playwright, APScheduler, Supabase, Pydantic |\n+| **Description** | Automated job search pipeline for international Physics teaching roles |\n \n ## Architecture Layers\n \n-The project is organised into 9 architectural layers with a strict fan-in/fan-out dependency structure. Configuration is consumed by all layers via `src/config.py`. The pipeline orchestrator (`src/main.py`) sequences all phases.\n+The project is organised into 9 layers with a strict fan-in/fan-out dependency structure. Configuration fans into all layers via `src/config.py` (8 incoming import edges). The pipeline orchestrator (`src/main.py`) has the highest fan-out (22 edges) connecting scrapers, storage, scoring, and notification modules.\n \n-```\n-  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n-  │                Documentation                     │\n-  │              (AGENTS.md)                         │\n-  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n-                         │ documents\n-  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n-  │          Pipeline Orchestration                  │\n-  │    src/main.py  ←→  src/scheduler/daily_run.py  │\n-  └──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────────┬──────────┘\n-         │      │      │      │          │\n-         ▼      ▼      ▼      ▼          ▼\n-  ┌────────┐ ┌──────┐ ┌────┐ ┌───────┐ ┌──────────┐\n-  │Scrapers│ │Score │ │Store│ │Notify │ │Extractors│\n-  │  (7)   │ │(LLM) │ │     │ │(Tg+  │ │(empty)   │\n-  │        │ │      │ │     │ │Email) │ │          │\n-  └────────┘ └──────┘ └────┘ └───────┘ └──────────┘\n-```\n+### 1. Configuration Layer\n+- **Files:** `.env`, `.gitignore`, `pyproject.toml`, `src/config.py`, `src/__init__.py`\n+- **Purpose:** Environment variables, project metadata, and Pydantic settings consumed by all downstream modules\n+- **Key module:** `src/config.py:Settings` loads API keys (Supabase, Groq, NVIDIA, OpenRouter, OpenCode Zen, Gemini, Telegram, Resend), provider preferences, schedule times, and notification config from `.env`\n \n-### Layer 1: Configuration\n-**Files:** `src/config.py`, `.env`, `pyproject.toml`\n+### 2. Pipeline Orchestration Layer\n+- **Files:** `src/main.py`, `src/scheduler/daily_run.py`, `src/scheduler/__init__.py`\n+- **Purpose:** Central orchestrator sequencing all 6 pipeline phases (scrape → pre-filter → dedup → upsert → score → notify) and APScheduler for automated daily runs\n+- **Key entrypoint:** `src/main.py:run_pipeline()`\n \n-Pydantic `Settings` class loads all API keys (Supabase, Groq, NVIDIA, OpenRouter, OpenCode Zen, Gemini, Telegram, Resend) and runtime settings from `.env`. Every downstream module imports this.\n+### 3. Data Collection Layer (Scrapers)\n+- **Files:** `src/scrapers/base.py` (abstract base), `src/scrapers/tes.py` (working), `src/scrapers/teacher_horizons.py` (bypassed), `src/scrapers/linkedin.py`, `schrole.py`, `search_associates.py` (placeholders)\n+- **Purpose:** All scraper implementations sharing the `BaseScraper` abstract class\n+- **Net import balance:** -5 (pure provider layer depended upon by orchestrator)\n \n-### Layer 2: Pipeline Orchestration\n-**Files:** `src/main.py`, `src/scheduler/daily_run.py`\n+### 4. Scoring Engine Layer\n+- **Files:** `src/scoring/llm_scorer.py`\n+- **Purpose:** Multi-provider LLM-powered job scoring against candidate profile with rule-based fallback\n+- **Consumes:** `data/profile.json` (candidate schema)\n \n-`run_pipeline()` is the central orchestrator sequencing 6 phases:\n-1. Scrape (runs all scrapers, collects job listings)\n-2. Heuristic pre-filter (drops jobs whose title/subject don't match physics/maths/science keywords)\n-3. Deduplicate (skips URLs already in Supabase or local `DedupStore`)\n-4. Upsert (stores new jobs in Supabase or local cache)\n-5. Score (LLMScorer with Groq primary, NVIDIA backup, rule-based fallback)\n-6. Notify (Telegram alerts + email digest for jobs ≥ 80)\n+### 5. Storage Layer\n+- **Files:** `src/storage/supabase_client.py`\n+- **Purpose:** Supabase CRUD on `job_listings` table; every method falls through to DedupStore SQLite on error\n+- **Backup store:** `data/dedup.db` (SQLite, tables: `seen_urls`, `job_cache`)\n \n-### Layer 3: Data Collection (Scrapers)\n-**Files:** `src/scrapers/base.py` (abstract base) + 5 scraper implementations  \n+### 6. Notification Layer\n+- **Files:** `src/notify/telegram.py`, `src/notify/email_sender.py`\n+- **Purpose:** Telegram Bot API alerts and HTML email digests (Resend or SMTP with STARTTLS)\n+- **Trigger:** Jobs scored ≥ 80\n \n-| Scraper | Status | Method |\n-|---------|--------|--------|\n-| TES | ✅ Working | Next.js `__NEXT_DATA__` JSON + HTML fallback |\n-| Libyan Jobs | ✅ Working | WordPress HTML parsing (`<article.loadmore-item.noo_job>`) |\n-| Teacher Horizons | ❌ Bypassed | Playwright SPA (requires activated account) |\n-| LinkedIn | 🔲 Placeholder | Async, returns `[]` |\n-| Schrole | 🔲 Placeholder | Requires login credentials |\n-| Search Associates | 🔲 Placeholder | Requires login credentials |\n+### 7. Data Schema Layer\n+- **Files:** `scripts/setup_supabase.sql`, `data/profile.json`\n+- **Purpose:** SQL migration defining `job_listings` table (UUID `id`, unique `url`, `score`, `notified`, indexes on score/notified/source/created_at) and candidate profile JSON schema\n \n-### Layer 4: Scoring Engine\n-**Files:** `src/scoring/llm_scorer.py`\n+### 8. Documentation Layer\n+- **Files:** `AGENTS.md`\n+- **Purpose:** Agent onboarding guide with pipeline details, entrypoints, gotchas, and configuration\n \n-Five-provider Weighted Provider Registry with health state machine:\n-- **Groq** (weight 40) — primary, batch-scoring, works\n-- **NVIDIA** (weight 30) — backup with thinking mode, works but slow\n-- **OpenRouter** (weight 20) — 429 rate-limited\n-- **OpenCode Zen** (weight 20) — 401 auth error\n-- **Gemini** (weight 15) — 429 quota exceeded\n+### 9. Extractors Layer (Placeholder)\n+- **Files:** `src/extractors/__init__.py`\n+- **Purpose:** Empty stub module planned for future extraction of structured data from scraped listings\n \n-Each provider has **intra-provider model rotation** (primary → fallback chain) with **tenacity exponential backoff** (3 attempts, `wait_random_exponential(multiplier=1, max=60)`). If all LLM providers fail, rule-based scoring (`_score_via_rules`) kicks in with base score 30.\n-\n-### Layer 5: Storage\n-**Files:** `src/storage/supabase_client.py`\n-\n-Dual-storage strategy: Supabase (primary) with automatic fallback to `DedupStore` SQLite at `data/dedup.db` when Supabase DNS is unreachable.\n-\n-### Layer 6: Notification\n-**Files:** `src/notify/telegram.py`, `src/notify/email_sender.py`\n-\n-- **Telegram Bot API** — sends job alerts with `scoring_reasoning` and cover letter files\n-- **Email** — Resend API or SMTP (STARTTLS) digest for jobs scored ≥ 80\n-\n-### Layer 7: Cover Letter Generator\n-**Files:** `src/generators/cover_letter.py`\n-\n-Generates personalised cover letters via Groq (primary) with cascade fallback through NVIDIA → OpenRouter → OpenCode Zen → Gemini → template fallback. Files saved as `school__title__LLM.md` or `school__title__fallback.md`.\n-\n-### Layer 8: Data Schema\n-**Files:** `scripts/setup_supabase.sql`, `data/profile.json`\n-\n-- `job_listings` table with indexes on `score`, `notified`, `source`, `created_at`\n-- Candidate profile in `data/profile.json` — target subjects, curricula, countries, salary expectations\n-\n-### Layer 9: Documentation\n-**Files:** `AGENTS.md`\n-\n-Comprehensive agent guide with entrypoints, pipeline details, gotchas, configuration, and relevant files. The single best resource for onboarding.\n-\n ## Key Concepts\n \n-### Weighted Provider Registry\n-No single \"primary\" provider is hardcoded. At startup, `validate_provider_configs()` probes every registered provider with a real scoring prompt. Providers that fail (401, 404, 429) are removed from rotation. Active providers are queried in descending weight order. Dead config: `SCORER_PROVIDER` and `SCORER_FALLBACK` env vars are not consumed by the scorer.\n+### Dual-Storage Architecture\n+Supabase is the primary database. When Supabase DNS is unreachable (`[Errno -2] Name or service not known`), ALL operations transparently fall through to a local SQLite store (`data/dedup.db`) with tables `seen_urls` (dedup) and `job_cache` (full job data + score + notified state). The dedup store persists across runs.\n \n-### Health State Machine\n-Each provider has three states:\n-- **ACTIVE** — ready for traffic\n-- **COOLING_DOWN** — hit an error, waits 300s then sends a canary probe\n-- **DEAD** — 3 consecutive cooldown failures, removed for the session\n+### URL-Based Deduplication\n+Jobs are deduplicated by their `url` field (the unique business key). The `upsert_jobs()` method uses `on_conflict=\"url\"` to handle duplicates. Both `update_score()` and `mark_notified()` filter by the `url` column, not the UUID `id` column.\n \n+### Multi-Provider LLM Scoring\n+The `LLMScorer` uses a Weighted Provider Registry that probes every registered provider at startup via `validate_provider_configs()`. Dead providers (401/404/429) are removed from rotation. The remaining providers are queried in weight order: Groq (40) → NVIDIA (30) → OpenRouter (20) → OpenCode Zen (20) → Gemini (15).\n+\n ### Intra-Provider Model Rotation\n-Before cascading to the next provider, each provider tries its own model chain internally:\n-- Groq: 1 model (from settings)\n-- NVIDIA: `nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b` (with thinking) → `nemotron-3-nano-30b-a3b`\n-- OpenCode Zen: `deepseek-v4-flash` → `deepseek-v4-pro`\n-- OpenRouter / Gemini: 1 model each\n+Before cascading to the next provider, each provider tries its own model chain internally with tenacity exponential backoff (3 attempts per model):\n+- **Groq:** 1 model from `settings.groq_model`\n+- **NVIDIA:** `nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b` (with thinking mode) → `nemotron-3-nano-30b-a3b`\n+- **OpenCode Zen:** `deepseek-v4-flash` → `deepseek-v4-pro` (both dead, 401)\n+- **OpenRouter / Gemini:** 1 model each\n \n-### Tenacity Exponential Backoff\n-Every API call is wrapped in `tenacity.Retrying` with `wait_random_exponential(multiplier=1, max=60)`, 3 attempts per model. Transient errors (429, 5xx, timeouts) trigger retries. Hard errors (401, 404) immediately advance to the next model.\n-\n ### Leaky-Bucket Pacing\n-Batch scoring splits jobs into chunks of `MAX_PER_CHUNK=5` with `CHUNK_DELAY=20` seconds between chunks. Total pipeline time ~3.5 minutes for 44 jobs (dominated by the 160s of accumulated delays).\n+Batch scoring splits jobs into chunks of `MAX_PER_CHUNK=5` with a `CHUNK_DELAY=20s` gap between chunks to stay under provider rate limits. Total pipeline time ~2.5-4 minutes for 20-44 jobs.\n \n-### Pipeline Logging Format\n-All provider attempts use a structured, grep-parseable format:\n-```\n-[PIPELINE] Provider: groq | Model: qwen/qwen3-32b | Result: Success | Attempt: 1\n-[PIPELINE] Provider: nvidia | Model: nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b | Result: Fail (HTTP 401) | Attempt: 2 — not retrying\n-```\n+### Rule-Based Fallback\n+If all LLM providers fail, `_score_via_rules` does keyword matching on subject, curriculum, country, and salary with a base score of 30.\n \n-### Dual-Storage Architecture\n-Supabase is the primary database. When DNS fails (`[Errno -2] Name or service not known`), ALL operations transparently fall through to a local SQLite store (`data/dedup.db`) with tables `seen_urls` (dedup) and `job_cache` (full job data + score + notified state).\n-\n ## Guided Tour\n \n ### Step 1: Project Overview\n-Start at **AGENTS.md**. This document describes the full pipeline, entrypoints, gotchas, and configuration. It's the single best onboarding resource and is kept in sync with the actual codebase.\n+Start at **AGENTS.md**. This document describes the full pipeline (scrape → deduplicate → upsert → score → notify), entrypoints, gotchas, and configuration. It's the single best onboarding resource.\n \n-### Step 2: Pipeline Entry Point\n-Open **src/main.py**. `run_pipeline()` is the central orchestrator with the highest fan-out (22 edges). It chains 6 phases linearly:\n-- `run_scrapers()` → `filter_relevant_jobs()` → dedup/upsert → `score_jobs_from_db()` or `score_jobs_in_memory()` → `send_notifications()`\n+### Step 2: Pipeline Entry Point & Orchestration\n+Open **src/main.py**. `run_pipeline()` is the central orchestrator with the highest fan-out (22 edges). It chains all phases:\n+- `run_scrapers()` → heuristic pre-filter → dedup via Supabase → upsert → `score_jobs_in_memory()` / `score_jobs_from_db()` → `send_notifications()`\n \n-Each phase is isolated in its own function, keeping the pipeline readable.\n+The CLI entry point `main()` calls `run_pipeline()` and prints a summary.\n \n ### Step 3: Configuration Foundation\n-Open **src/config.py**. The `Settings` class uses Pydantic `BaseSettings` to load everything from `.env`. It has the highest fan-in (8 incoming edges) — every module imports it. Key settings: API keys, provider models, notification config, schedule times.\n+Open **src/config.py**. The `Settings` class uses Pydantic `BaseSettings` to load API keys, provider preferences, and notification config from `.env`. It has the highest fan-in (8 incoming edges) — every module imports it.\n \n-### Step 4: Scraper Foundation\n-Open **src/scrapers/base.py**. `BaseScraper` is an abstract base class providing shared HTTP client setup, BeautifulSoup4 parsing helpers, and URL utilities. All 5 scraper implementations inherit from it and must implement `scrape()`.\n+### Step 4: Scraper Foundation & Abstract Base\n+Open **src/scrapers/base.py**. `BaseScraper` is the abstract base class with the second-highest fan-in (7 inheriting classes). It provides shared HTTP client setup, BeautifulSoup4 parsing helpers, and URL utilities. All 5 scraper implementations inherit from it and must implement `scrape()`.\n \n-### Step 5: Working Scraper — TES\n-Open **src/scrapers/tes.py`. This is the primary working scraper. It first attempts to parse the Next.js `__NEXT_DATA__` JSON embedded in the page, falling back to HTML parsing. It infers curriculum (IB, GCSE, IGCSE, A-Level) from job details and constructs absolute URLs from relative `canonicalUrl` fields.\n+### Step 5: Working Scraper: TES\n+Open **src/scrapers/tes.py**. `TESScraper` is the primary working scraper. It first attempts to parse the Next.js `__NEXT_DATA__` JSON embedded in the page, falling back to HTML parsing. It infers curriculum (IB, GCSE, IGCSE, A-Level) from job details and constructs absolute URLs from relative `canonicalUrl` fields.\n \n-### Step 6: Scoring Engine\n-Open **src/scoring/llm_scorer.py`. This is the most complex module:\n-- `LLMScorer.__init__` builds a provider list from non-empty API keys, orders by weight\n-- `validate_provider_configs()` probes each provider within a 2-minute deadline\n-- `_try_models()` implements per-provider model rotation with tenacity retries\n-- `score()` cascades through active providers with rule-based ultimate fallback\n-- `batch_score()` handles leaky-bucket pacing\n+### Step 6: Additional Scrapers\n+`TeacherHorizonsScraper` uses Playwright for Angular SPA rendering but is **bypassed** because detail pages require an activated teacher account. `LinkedInScraper`, `SchroleScraper`, and `SearchAssociatesScraper` are placeholders requiring login credentials — they log warnings and return empty lists.\n \n-### Step 7: Notification\n-Open **src/notify/telegram.py** and **src/notify/email_sender.py**. The Telegram module sends formatted job alerts with `scoring_reasoning`. The email module supports dual-transport: Resend API or direct SMTP with STARTTLS.\n+### Step 7: Scoring Engine\n+Open **src/scoring/llm_scorer.py**. The `LLMScorer` probes multiple LLM providers at startup, caches working providers, and scores jobs against the candidate profile (`data/profile.json`). If all LLM providers fail, `_score_via_rules` applies keyword matching with a base score of 30.\n \n-### Step 8: Storage\n-Open **src/storage/supabase_client.py**. The `SupabaseClient` wraps CRUD on the `job_listings` table. Every method catches `httpx.HTTPError` and falls through to `DedupStore` (SQLite). The SQL migration in `scripts/setup_supabase.sql` must be applied manually before first run.\n+### Step 8: Notifications\n+Open **src/notify/telegram.py** and **src/notify/email_sender.py**. Telegram sends formatted job alerts via the Bot API. Email provides dual-transport digests via Resend API or direct SMTP with STARTTLS. Both are invoked for jobs scored ≥ 80.\n \n-### Step 9: Scheduler\n-Open **src/scheduler/daily_run.py**. Uses APScheduler `BlockingScheduler` with `CronTrigger` to run `run_pipeline()` at configurable daily times (`.env` `SCHEDULE_TIME`). Runs as a background process via `nohup`, not as an OS cron job.\n+### Step 9: Database Storage & Schema\n+Open **src/storage/supabase_client.py**. `SupabaseClient` wraps CRUD on the `job_listings` table. Every method catches `httpx.HTTPError` and falls through to `DedupStore` (SQLite at `data/dedup.db`). Open `scripts/setup_supabase.sql` — this migration must be applied manually via the Supabase SQL Editor before the first run.\n \n-### Step 10: Cover Letters\n-Open **src/generators/cover_letter.py`. Generates personalised cover letters by probing providers in order: Groq → NVIDIA → OpenRouter → OpenCode Zen → Gemini. Has a diversity evaluation loop (`_run_evaluation_loop`) that checks sentence overlap < 40% with `time.sleep(5)` between calls. Letters are tagged `__LLM` (AI-generated) or `__fallback` (template).\n+### Step 10: Scheduler\n+Open **src/scheduler/daily_run.py**. Uses APScheduler `BlockingScheduler` to trigger `run_pipeline()` at configurable daily times (`SCHEDULE_TIME` in `.env`, e.g. `08:00,17:00,22:00`). Runs as a background process via `nohup`, not as an OS cron job.\n \n ## File Map\n \n ### Configuration Layer\n | File | Purpose |\n |------|---------|\n | `src/config.py` | Pydantic Settings — all API keys, provider models, notification config |\n | `.env` | Live credentials (Supabase, Groq, NVIDIA, Telegram, email) |\n-| `pyproject.toml` | Dependencies, CLI entry points |\n+| `pyproject.toml` | Project metadata, dependencies, CLI scripts |\n \n ### Pipeline Orchestration Layer\n | File | Purpose |\n |------|---------|\n | `src/main.py` | Central pipeline — `run_pipeline()` sequences all 6 phases |\n | `src/scheduler/daily_run.py` | APScheduler — triggers pipeline at configurable daily times |\n \n-### Scrapers Layer\n-| File | Purpose |\n-|------|---------|\n-| `src/scrapers/base.py` | Abstract base — HTTP client, BS4 parsing, URL utilities |\n-| `src/scrapers/tes.py` | ✅ Working — Next.js JSON + HTML fallback, curriculum inference |\n-| `src/scrapers/libyan_jobs.py` | (added later) WordPress scraper, selector `article.loadmore-item.noo_job` |\n-| `src/scrapers/teache