        "text": "Two questions, plain answers:\n\n**How the scheduler works:** It's a Python script (`src/scheduler/daily_run.py`) that uses APScheduler — a library that acts like an alarm clock inside Python. When you start it with `nohup ... &`, it runs in the **background** as a persistent process, checks the current time every minute, and fires `run_pipeline()` at whatever times you set in `.env`'s `SCHEDULE_TIME` (e.g. 08:00, 17:00, 22:00). It doesn't use OS cron — it's pure Python running in a loop.\n\n**If you close your laptop:** Yes, it **will stop**. `nohup` only keeps it alive across terminal/logout boundaries, but when the machine suspends (lid close) or shuts down, the process dies. It will not restart automatically when you reopen the lid. You'd need to run the `nohup` command again after resuming.\n\nIf you want it to survive reboots and lid closes, you need either a VPS (always-on server) or a tool like `systemd` / `pm2` / `screen` with auto-restart. Want me to set up a `systemd` user service for this?",
